\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-\uc2 Thai c.bank\uc2  sees lower H1 economic growth due to Omicron outbreak\b0\par\par\uc2 417 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 2022\par 07:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Updates with quotes in paragraphs 4 and 9, detail on policy meetings)\par \par \uc2 BANGKOK, Jan 10 (Reuters) - \uc2 Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Monday the country's economy could see lower growth in the first half of 2022 than a baseline scenario due to uncertainties caused by the Omicron variant of the coronavirus.\par \par \uc2 While Southeast Asia's second-biggest economy has been supported by domestic spending and a rebound in tourism, a growing outbreak of Omicron remained a key risk, \uc2 the Bank of Thailand\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Thailand is expected to bring the Omicron outbreak under control within the first quarter of 2022, it said.\par \par \uc2 "There are great downside risks in the first half of 2022 because of the Omicron outbreak, which could be more severe and longer than expected," Sakkapop Panyanukul, a senior director at the Bank of Thailand, told a meeting with analysts.\par \par \uc2 Thailand's economy is forecast to expand by 3.4% in 2022, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said last week.\par \par \uc2 Financial stability for households and businesses remained fragile, reflected by high levels of household and corporate debt, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed.\par \par \uc2 Non-performing loans (NPL) were seen increasing slightly, Sakkapol said, adding that the bank will roll out targeted measures in this area.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is also seen increasing temporarily in the first half of 2022, but would still be within target, Piti Disyatat, an assistant governor at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , told the meeting.\par \par \uc2 "If inflation needs to be managed, increasing interest rates will be our main tool," Piti said.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets if the baht \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  became too volatile, he said.\par \par \uc2 The baht hit 33.14 against the U.S. dollar on Jan. 5, the highest since late November, though eased to 33.67 on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy committee last month kept the benchmark interest rate at a record low of 0.50% for a 13th consecutive meeting to support the economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Going forward, the committee will only meet six times a year, or every seven to 10 weeks, down from eight times or every six to eight weeks previously.\par \par \uc2 "Meeting frequently creates unnecessary burden for the financial market to anticipate what is coming," said Piti.\par \par \uc2 The committee is due to meet for the first time this year on Feb. 9. (Reporting by Satawasin Staporncharnchai; Writing by Patpicha Tanakasempipat; Editing by Ed Davies) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-1-10T07:08:59.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220110ei1a00nax\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
