\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Thai baht, Indonesian rupiah lead Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  higher\b0\par\par\uc2 688 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2021\par 06:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Thai baht near two-month high\par     * Philippine peso slips\par     * S.Korea's won set to soften for third straight month\par     * Malaysian markets closed for a holiday\par \par     By Sameer Manekar\par     Aug 31 (Reuters) - Most Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  firmed on Tuesday,\par with the Thai baht and Indonesian rupiah leading the pack, as\par the U.S. dollar remained under pressure from waning conviction\par about any immediate \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  stimulus tapering.\par     The baht extended its gains into a third\par consecutive session, appreciating as much as 0.4% to hit a near\par two-month high, while the Indonesian rupiah rose for a\par second day to scale a near one-month high.\par     The U.S. dollar index hovered near its two-week lows\par against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , stabilising from falls after\par Powell's speech last week that offered no clear signs on the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's plan to cut its asset purchases.\par     "Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be in a good position this week, with\par risk environment leaning broadly risk-on, and the market still\par digesting Powell's dovish leanings," analysts at Singapore-bank\par OCBC said in a note.\par     The next thing on investors' radar is the U.S. non-farm\par payrolls data due later in the week. A weaker August jobs\par numbers could solidify the case for the Fed prolonging its\par dovish stance.\par     The baht was further supported by Thailand's cabinet\par approving $1.37 billion in relief measures on Monday to counter\par the impact of the COVID-19 outbreak as data on Monday showed\par factory output growth slowed down in July.\par \par     "In the near-term we could see the Thai baht rally toward\par the strong support zone around 32.25 from more foreign fund\par inflows in Thai assets, especially equities due to optimism over\par the COVID situation," Poon Panichpibool, markets strategists at\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Krung Thai Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  said.\par     "However, the fast rally of the baht recently could urge \f28 \uc2 \f2 the\par Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market to lower the\par volatility," Panichpibool added.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was set to appreciate more than 1% in August,\par while equities in Bangkok, which traded nearly flat on\par Tuesday, were on track to end the month nearly 8% stronger.\par     Indonesia's rupiah was also set to add more than 1%\par in August, with the shares in Jakarta eyeing a more than\par 3% gain over the month.\par     The Philippine peso, the outlier in the region,\par declined 0.3% on Tuesday but was set to add 0.4% in August.\par South Korea's won, on the other hand, was on a path\par to depreciate for a third straight month.\par     Elsewhere, equities largely traded in the red, with\par Singapore shares declining the most to hit their lowest\par since mid-May as weak factory activity data from China, the\par region's biggest trading partner, showing signs of increasing\par economic pressures.\par     Markets in Malaysia were closed for a\par holiday.\par     \par     HIGHLIGHTS:    \par     ** Indonesian 10-year benchmark yields fall 2.0 basis points\par to 6.121%\par     ** China's multi-pronged crackdown on its tech companies -\par \par     ** S. Korea drafts aggressive spending plan for 2022, taking\par debt to 50% of GDP -\par     \par   Asia stock indexes and                            \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 0342 GMT                        \par  COUNTRY  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RIC        \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   INDE  STOCK  STOCK\par                      DAILY   YTD     X      S  S YTD\par                          %     %        DAILY      %\par                                             %  \par  Japan               +0.05  -6.0  <.N2   0.3   1.56\par                                3  25>          \par  China    <CNY=CFX   -0.02  +0.9  <.SS  -0.87   0.71\par           S>                   2  EC>          \par  India               +0.00  -0.2  <.NS   0.00  21.09\par                                7  EI>          \par  Indones             +0.30  -1.9  <.JK  -0.27   2.50\par  ia                            9  SE>          \par  Philipp             -0.15  -3.5  <.PS  -1.48  -6.35\par  ines                          6  I>           \par  S.Korea  <KRW=KFT   +0.30  -6.6  <.KS   0.48   9.95\par           C>                   4  11>          \par  Singapo             +0.09  -1.7  <.ST  -1.16   7.82\par  re                            3  I>           \par  Taiwan              +0.21  +2.6  <.TW  -0.69  17.27\par                                2  II>          \par  Thailan             +0.41  -7.3  <.SE   0.09  12.83\par  d                             6  TI>          \par  \par     \par  (Reporting by Sameer Manekar in Bengaluru; Editing by Sam\par Holmes)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-8-31T05:18:52.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020210831eh8v00ify\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
