\par\par \b \uc2 THB Weakness May Draw Verbal  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  From Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Thailand \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,277 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2021\par 05:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0344 GMT - Weakness in the Thai baht may draw verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \uc2 the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Wednesday's rate decision, analysts say, noting that THB is the weakest Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  year to date. With the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  widely expected to keep rates on hold, possible \uc2 BoT\uc2  comments on the baht will likely be more interesting, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. There could be some jawboning remarks to at least mitigate or slow the pace of THB depreciation, as an uncontrolled selloff could dent investor sentiment, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. USD/THB is up 0.2% at 33.09 after earlier touching 33.10, the highest intraday level since April 2020, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0305 GMT - China's economic growth could be whittled down around 0.7 percentage point by the latest Delta outbreak if the scope of government restrictions remained in place for one month, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says. The investment bank predicts China's services activity will take the brunt of the impact this time, though the actual outcome could be varied depending on the ultimate length and breadth of the outbreak. GS currently holds its forecast of China's 3Q GDP growth unchanged at 6.2%, as Beijing will soon release economic data in the coming days. (singaporeeditors@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT - JPY strengthens against most G-10 and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid continuing worries over the spread of the Delta variant of Covid-19 in Asia. Chinese media are reporting that 31 provincial-level regions are warning against travel, suggesting that residents remain at home given the Delta variant's outbreak, \uc2 NAB\uc2  says. There are also concerns that China's domestic vaccines are less effective against the Delta variant, \uc2 NAB\uc2  adds. USD/JPY falls 0.1% to 108.95, SGD/JPY drops 0.1% to 80.65 and AUD/JPY is down 0.1% at 80.53. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0145 GMT - Lockdowns hit Australian retail sales hard in June, and far worse is expected in July and August. Retail sales fell 1.8% in June from May. The outcome was heavily influenced by stay-at-home directives in multiple states and territories, with large falls experienced by cafes, restaurants and takeaway food services, clothing, footwear and personal accessory retailing, and department stores. The hit to spending in the months ahead is expected to be significant given Sydney's lockdown is set to continue until the end of the month, adding to the prospect of a contraction in the broader economy. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0140 GMT - NZD/USD is up 0.4% at 0.7048 and NZD/AUD rises 0.5% to 0.9529 as \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  rate increase expectations are frontloaded into the second half of this year following strong employment growth. Banks now expect 75 bps of \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  rate increases by December, up from previous expectations of 50 bps. The unemployment rate dropped to a much lower-than-expected 4.0% in 2Q, which could stoke inflation pressures that are already becoming elevated by local standards. Rate-sensitive stocks on the NZX-50 are higher despite the potential for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise its cash rate as soon as this month. The index adds 0.3% to 12737 in early afternoon trading. (stephen.wright@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0046 GMT - Lockdowns hit Australian services activity hard in July, with the IHS Markit Australia Services PMI falling to 44.2 in from 56.8 in June. The drop puts an end to the 10-month growth streak and marks the sharpest decline in services output since May 2020. The drop in services will further reinforce expectations that the economy will contract sharply in the third quarter. The economy could be sluggish well into 4Q as lockdowns in Sydney, a services hub, are expected to drag on.(james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 2340 GMT - The big drop in New Zealand's unemployment rate in 2Q to 4.0% means some banks are now forecasting \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  to raise its cash rate three times this year instead of two, to counter inflation pressures. That scenario would take the cash rate to 1.0% by December from its current record-low of 0.25%, according to \uc2 ASB\uc2 , ANZ and \uc2 BNZ\uc2 . The "sheer strength" of the labor market indicates the \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  is likely to lift the cash rate relatively swiftly in coming months, \uc2 ASB\uc2  says. It expects a slower pace of rate increases next year, with the cash rate likely at 1.5% by the end of 2022. (stephen.wright@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2207 GMT - The \uc2 RBA\uc2 's relatively hawkish stance won't be enough to prevent further falls in the Australian dollar, \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says. The \uc2 RBA\uc2  signaled yesterday it would proceed with tapering its asset purchases later this year, confounding expectations among economists that it would delay this plan due to Covid-19 lockdowns in Australia. "The bigger picture is that uncertainty about both the domestic recovery and the global outlook will probably continue to weigh on Australia's government bond yields and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says. At 0.7395, the AUD/USD remains a touch above an eight-month low achieved in July. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 2102 GMT - The IPC stock index closes up 1.5% to 51633, with beverages and retail conglomerate \uc2 Femsa\uc2  advancing 3.2% and \uc2 America Movil\uc2  shares up 1.9%. The peso firms to 19.88 against the US dollar from 19.91 yesterday. The yield on 28-day Treasury bills slipped 2bp to 4.33% at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weekly auction. Short-term debt valuations already reflect expectations of further monetary tightening ahead, Banorte says. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2053 GMT - The US dollar moves little, with the WSJ Dollar Index slightly down. The dollar weakens 0.3% versus the yen and 0.2% against the pound, and is a little stronger versus the euro. Markets juggle opposing expectations about Friday's nonfarm payrolls. A positive surprise could boost the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by fanning forecasts of speedy monetary tightening, while too few new openings would hint to a slower recovery, delayed Fed action and less of a support for the dollar. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 2011 GMT - Mexican consumers were more upbeat in July about the current economic situation compared with a year ago than they had been in June, but were less optimistic about prospects a year from now, leaving the consumer confidence index unchanged at 44.3, statistics institute Inegi reports. The perceived ability to buy durable goods rose slightly from June, but remained low at 24.4, where 50 marks a line between optimism and pessimism. The overall index could hit 50 for the first time if the economic recovery maintains its momentum, brokerage Monex says, "but for now it seems more likely that it will stabilize around these levels." (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1454 GMT - New orders for factory goods in the U.S. increased 1.5% in June on-month, according to data from the Commerce Department. The reading is above the 1% increase expected by economists polled by The Wall Street Journal and follows an upwardly revised 2.3% rise in May. New orders for manufactured durable goods rose 0.9% in June, up from the preliminary estimate and compared with a 3.2% rise in May. Factories across the U.S. can't keep up with strong demand as supply-chain disruptions and labor shortages hit production. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 03, 2021 23:49 ET (03:49 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020210804eh8400098\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
