\par\par \b \uc2 Thai economy ends gloomy year with slim prospects for cheer in 2020\b0\par\par\uc2 608 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2019\par 10:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Growth in 2019 seen slowest in 5 years\par \par \uc2 * Exports to remain weak, baht \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to outperform\par \par \uc2 * No positive signs for economy yet - industry executive\par \par \uc2 By Orathai Sriring and Satawasin Staporncharnchai\par \par \uc2 BANGKOK, Dec 30 (Reuters) - Thailand is set to end 2019 at the weakest pace of growth in five years and little to cheer about next year, as Southeast Asia's second-largest economy faces headwinds from global trade tensions, a surging baht and rising political risks.\par \par \uc2 The export-reliant country has been sharply hit by the Sino-U.S. trade conflict. Exports may fall 3.3% in 2019 before rising just 0.5% in 2020, according to \uc2 the Bank of Thailand\uc2  (BOT).\par \par \uc2 A strong Thai Baht, which has gained 8.3% against the dollar in 2019 and is Asia's top-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has added to the pressure on exports. Analysts say it could also hit tourism.\par \par \uc2 Thailand's growth has lagged peers for years, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , after several downgrades, predicts it will be just 2.5% this year, the weakest pace since 2014 when the army seized power in a coup, and forecasts it would be 2.8% in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts are even more pessimistic.\par \par \uc2 "We see growth of just 2.4% this year and 2.5% next year," said Somprawin Manprasert, chief economist of Bank of Ayudhya. "The economy is still in a slowdown," he said, adding that poor exports are now hurting domestic activity.\par \par \uc2 Charnon Boonnuch, an economist at Nomura in Singapore, said he only expected a sluggish economic recovery in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Thailand is a regional production and export base for global carmakers, but car shipments fell 6% in the first 11 months of 2019, prompting some factories to cut work hours, said Surapong Paisitpattanapong, spokesman of the Federation of Thai Industries' auto division.\par \par \uc2 "A profit from making one car is no more than 5%, but our baht has gained 7%-8%. So the more they export, the more they suffer losses," he said.\par \par \uc2 In a bid to fight \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength, the BOT imposed various steps and cut its key rate twice in 2019 to a record low of 1.25%, but the baht remains firm, driven by the large current account surplus.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said that more measures are possible, adding that market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be difficult as \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks being added to a U.S. watchlist of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators.\par \par \uc2 The government has been trying to lift growth by infusing more funds, including a $10 billion stimulus package, but with little impact.\par \par \uc2 There was also a four-month delay in the 2020 budget to February due to a delay in cabinet formation, after an election in March voted former junta leader \uc2 Prayuth Chan-ocha\uc2  back to office as prime minister with a slim majority in parliament.\par \par \uc2 Political uncertainty is rising after thousands of people recently joined the biggest protest since Prayuth's 2014 coup, following a move by authorities to ban a party that has rallied opposition to the government.\par \par \uc2 In January, a court will rule on the dissolution of the Future Forward Party, which could again spark protests.\par \par \uc2 "The economy should be better next year, albeit not much, if there is no fresh political chaos," said Sanan Angubolkul, vice chairman of the Board of Trade of Thailand.\par \par \uc2 Visit Limluecha, vice chairman of the Thai National Shippers' Council, said there are no positive signs for the economy yet. "I see no light at the end of the tunnel."\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Kitiphong Thaichareon; Editing by Matthew Tostevin and Rashmi Aich) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-12-30T10:01:15.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020191230efcu00se9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
