\par\par \b \uc2 BREAKINGVIEWS-Baht\u8217\'20\'19s brawn weakens sick man of Southeast Asia\b0\par\par\uc2 629 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 2019\par 06:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (The author is a Reuters Breakingviews columnist. The opinions expressed are her own.)\par \par \uc2 By Clara Ferreira-Marques\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Aug 19 (Reuters Breakingviews) - Thailand's outperforming \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is battering the sick man of Southeast Asia. The $500 billion economy expanded at its slowest annual rate in almost five years in the second quarter. A stubbornly strong baht is hardly helping. Bangkok can do more to ease the pressure: spending, tempering inflows, and pushing its hefty domestic funds to venture abroad.\par \par \uc2 Official figures on Monday showed an economy cooling fast: it grew a weaker-than-expected 2.3% from a year earlier, slowing from the first quarter. A trade war may eventually bring in businesses relocating from China to avoid U.S. levies, but for now tariffs have battered exports, particularly automotive sales. Consumption remains unimpressive, a drought is parching the country, and tourism has disappointed.\par \par \uc2 The baht\u8217\'20\'19s popularity is making those problems worse. While emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have largely softened as interest rates fall and tensions between Washington and Beijing rise, the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now at its strongest level in six years. Investors have made it an unwilling safe haven, piling into its bonds and betting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep rising. At the same time, domestic funds are staying home.\par \par \uc2 Massaging the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is easier said than done. Politics are not supportive. Spending on big projects that might have increased imports has slowed under a rickety coalition; the 2020 budget remains snarled. A $10 billion stimulus package announced last week is more cash handouts than new bridges.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s push to weaken the U.S. dollar isn\u8217\'20\'19t helpful either. Thai officials will be wary of sliding towards capital controls, and of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy greenbacks: \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to avoid being named a manipulator by a cantankerous White House.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut the benchmark interest rate 25 basis points to 1.5% earlier this month. It has room to do more there - and beyond. It has already sought to cool speculative inflows by tackling non-residents' baht accounts, and might restrict bond supply too. Officials, meanwhile, could push conservative Thai funds to invest more in faster growing neighbours, as Japan Inc. does. Large corporates like Thai Bev and others are already doing so. If pension and investment funds follow, the brawny baht might weaken at last.\par \par \uc2 On \uc2 Twitter{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/claramarquesrtr"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/claramarquesrtr}}}\par \par \uc2 CONTEXT NEWS\par \par \uc2 - Thailand\u8217\'20\'19s gross domestic product expanded 2.3% from a year earlier in the second quarter, the National Economic and Social Development Board said on Aug. 19, marking the weakest annual pace in almost five years.\par \par \uc2 - A Reuters poll of 12 economists, published on Aug. 15, had forecast 2.4% year-on-year growth for the April to June quarter, the weakest pace since the last quarter of 2014.\par \par \uc2 - The national planning agency cut its 2019 GDP growth forecast to a range of 2.7% to 3.2%, versus 3.3% to 3.8% projected in May.\par \par \uc2 - Thailand\u8217\'20\'19s finance minister said on Aug. 16 that the country was planning a stimulus package worth $10 billion that would include support for farmers and tourism.\par \par \uc2 - \uc2 Thailand\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Aug. 7 unexpectedly cut its benchmark interest rate for the first time since 2011 by 25 basis points, to 1.5%.\par \par \uc2 - The baht was trading on Aug. 19 at 30.86 to the dollar. So far this year, the baht has strengthened just over 5%, making it the top-performer among Asia\u8217\'20\'19s emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 - For previous columns by the author, Reuters customers can click on\par \par \uc2 - SIGN UP FOR BREAKINGVIEWS EMAIL ALERTS: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://bit.ly/BVsubscribe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://bit.ly/BVsubscribe}}}\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Pete Sweeney and Sharon Lam) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-8-19T05:46:23.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190819ef8j00ga5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
