\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Thailand not a  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  manipulator, says c.bank chief\b0\par\par\uc2 336 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2019\par 09:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, quotes)\par \par \uc2 By Kitiphong Thaichareon\par \par \uc2 BANGKOK, May 16 (Reuters) - Thailand does not manipulate the value of the baht \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to gain any competitive advantage for exporters, its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said on Thursday in response to speculation Thailand could be added to a U.S. watchlist.\par \par \uc2 "If we had done so, our baht would not be like this," Bank of Thailand Governor \uc2 Veerathai Santiprabhob\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The baht has risen over 3% against the dollar since the end of last year, making it the best-performing emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia.\par \par \uc2 The baht's strength has been driven by external factors and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will remain volatile due to global trade tensions and the monetary policies of major countries, Veerathai said.\par \par \uc2 He said the only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  undertaken was aimed at smoothing out any excessive moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Thailand's had a high current account surplus and a trade surplus with the United States without the benefit of any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market manipulation, he said, and there should be no reason for alarm over reports that Thailand could be added to a list of countries being monitored by the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Do no panic if you hear that we are added to the watchlist. It will not affect the economy and our policy implementation," he said.\par \par \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reduce volatility when large foreign fund inflows hit the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Veerathai said.\par \par \uc2 "That's normal, like other emerging countries," he said.\par \par \uc2 Thailand's economic growth this year is expected to be less than the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's forecast of 3.8 percent as rising trade protectionism hurt exports and investment, Veerathai said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will next review its growth forecast at its monetary policy meeting in June. Growth last year was 4.1 percent, the fastest in six years. (Writing by Orathai Sriring Editing by Simon Cameron-Moore) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-5-16T08:45:28.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190516ef5g015ii\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
