\par\par \b \uc2 Thailand invites foreign issuers to cool baht\b0\par\par\uc2 693 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 April 2018\par 10:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Bonds: Remittance rules remain a challenge for overseas borrowers\par \par \uc2 By Kit Yin Boey\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, April 13 (IFR) - Thailand is once again turning to foreign bond issuers in an effort to cool the strengthening baht, as the country attempts to dodge being named on a list of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators being compiled by the US.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has moved to attract more baht borrowings from foreign issuers, extending the regular March application window to April 20 and allowing the proceeds to be taken out of the country, provided they are exchanged into US dollars onshore first.\par \par \uc2 The policy shift is the latest relaxation of a move in early 2016 to ban any foreign issuer from bringing the proceeds of a baht bond sale out of the country. It comes after Thai authorities last September began allowing exceptions on a case-by-case basis, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  Laos being the first foreign issuer to obtain approval to repatriate a portion of a Bt14bn bond sold in Thailand in October.\par \par \uc2 The looser policy is aimed at curbing the baht's appreciation against the US dollar, after a 13% gain since the start of 2017. The baht hit a 4.5-year high at 31.07 to the dollar on March 14 and was last quoted at 31.13.\par \par \uc2 Thailand is widely touted as a potential candidate for the \uc2 US Treasury\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s expected list of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators, which it defines by three criteria: a current surplus of more than 3% of GDP, a significant bilateral trade surplus with the US and a track record of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The South-East Asian country\u8217\'20\'19s current account surplus was 10.3% of its GDP last year while its trade surplus was more than US$20bn.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThailand is at borderline risk of meeting all three criteria should the \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  opt to include it in its analysis for the first time,\u8221\'20\'1D wrote Vijay Patel, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ING.\par \par \uc2 Ironically, the appreciating baht could just be the factor to tip in Thailand\u8217\'20\'19s favour.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAlternatively, we note that it could give \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  a 'hall pass' on the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  front \u8211\'20\'13 not least given that the Thai baht has appreciated by more than 13% against the USD since 2017,\u8221\'20\'1D said Patel.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  ALTERNATIVES\par \par \uc2 The Thai government has been at pains to avoid any move that could be interpreted as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation. In mid-January, MOF officials stressed that it had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and would not, thereby forcing it to look at other ways to curb the strong baht.\par \par \uc2 Previous moves to allow foreign issuers to take the funds out in the spot market had limited success in attracting issuers, and bankers do not think the latest round will be any different. The special window in September yielded only one approval (for a Bt20bn issue from \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 ) but the offering ultimately did not materialise.\par \par \uc2 Issuers told PDMO at that time the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates were not in their favour to take the additional risks of an exchange in the spot market. While issuers can hedge both coupon and principal payments in the swap market, they are unable to do so in an onshore spot market. This raises \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks for them when meeting regular and principal payment obligations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAllowing foreign issuers to exchange on the spot market to help keep the baht down is just not an effective tool to deal with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange rates,\u8221\'20\'1D said a local banker.\par \par \uc2 Other bankers agree, noting that only a handful of foreign issuers sell bonds in the Thai market each year, and that potential issuers have voiced concerns over the same issues as well.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBorrowers like the Laos sovereign could cope with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks as they had only brought out a very small portion of the bond proceeds out of the country,\u8221\'20\'1D said one originations banker. \u8220\'20\'1CBut if the proceeds are larger, the risks will be too high.\u8221\'20\'1D (This story will appear in the April 14 issue of IFR Asia magazine. Reporting by Kit Yin Boey; Editing by Daniel Stanton and \uc2 Steve Garton\uc2  )\par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-4-16T08:43:06.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180413ee4d00c6x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
