\par\par \b \uc2 BREAKINGVIEWS-Thai baht races ahead of economic upgrades\b0\par\par\uc2 594 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2017\par 04:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (The author is a Reuters Breakingviews columnist. The opinions expressed are his own.)\par \par \uc2 By William Pesek\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 12 (Reuters Breakingviews) - The baht is posing problems for Thailand again, 20 years after a sudden devaluation sparked chaos worldwide. Today, Asia\u8217\'20\'19s best-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is too strong for a country that derives 70 percent of economic activity from exports, and which must fend off competitive threats from China to India.\par \par \uc2 There are various forces behind the baht's 8.1 percent surge this year. Investors are losing faith in Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s economic agenda and, in turn, the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is loath to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fearing the U.S. president would label \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator. Meanwhile, relatively high yields on Thai assets are luring speculators.\par \par \uc2 Graphic: Year-to-date percentage change in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  versus the U.S. dollar: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://reut.rs/2f0Qlel"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://reut.rs/2f0Qlel}}}\par \par \uc2 Throw in a booming tourist trade, rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and a current-account surplus, and the baht is a buy, at least for now. But the fundamental backdrop is weaker. Three-plus years of opaque military rule might normally deter investors. The deeper problem is the glacial pace of structural upgrades needed to increase innovation and productivity, attract foreign-direct investment and avoid the dreaded middle-income trap.\par \par \uc2 In 2014, the junta led by General Prayuth Chan-ocha grabbed power pledging to steady a nation plagued by legislative gridlock and massive street protests after 10 leadership changes in as many years. Prayuth promised the get-things-done competence that military leaders often use to justify a coup.\par \par \uc2 But things haven\u8217\'20\'19t gone to plan. Thailand's 3.7 percent year-on-year growth, which lags many peers, is the product of external factors and populist pump-priming. The junta has moved slowly on an ambitious $45 billion programme to upgrade roads, ports, and power grids, which is vital to encouraging the Toyotas and Samsungs of the world to create more Thai jobs. Efforts to eradicate corruption, cut red tape and reduce bank bad-loan ratios worse than China\u8217\'20\'19s have been tepid. And the workforce lacks the education needed to lift per-capita income above $10,000 from today\u8217\'20\'19s $6,000. As a result, private-sector investment is lagging.\par \par \uc2 In the long run, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is only strong as the foundations underpinning it. This junta has overseen the longest stretch of military rule since the early 1970s. If it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t put some big reforms on the scoreboard, the baht could fall foul of economic gravity.\par \par \uc2 On \uc2 Twitter{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/williampesek"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/williampesek}}}\par \par \uc2 CONTEXT NEWS\par \par \uc2 - Thailand\u8217\'20\'19s military has staged 12 coups since the end of direct rule by kings in 1932, the latest on May 22, 2014.\par \par \uc2 - The economy\u8217\'20\'19s better-than-expected 3.7 percent growth in the second quarter from a year earlier lagged China\u8217\'20\'19s 6.9 percent, the Philippines\u8217\'20\'19 6.5 percent and Indonesia\u8217\'20\'19s 5.0 percent.\par \par \uc2 - On Aug. 21, \uc2 the Bank of Thailand\uc2  asked commercial banks for data on an \u8220\'20\'1Cunusually high volume of transfers between non-resident baht accounts which may relate to baht speculation\u8221\'20\'1D and warned it might \u8220\'20\'1Cconsider additional measures to limit the opportunity\u8221\'20\'1D to drive the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher.\par \par \uc2 - William Pesek is a Tokyo-based journalist, former columnist for Barron\u8217\'20\'19s and Bloomberg and author of \u8220\'20\'1CJapanization: What the World Can Learn from Japan\u8217\'20\'19s Lost Decades.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 - SIGN UP FOR BREAKINGVIEWS EMAIL ALERTS {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://bit.ly/BVsubscribe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://bit.ly/BVsubscribe}}}\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Quentin Webb and Katrina Hamlin)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-9-15T02:23:44.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170912ed9c0030d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
