\par\par \b \uc2 Thailand says US trade deficit "not significant", no unfair policy\b0\par\par\uc2 371 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Mai 2017\par 14:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK, May 11 (Reuters) - Thailand said on Thursday the U.S. trade deficit with it was "not significant" and it had not conducted any \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate policies to gain an unfair trade advantage.\par \par \uc2 Thailand is among countries that run a trade surplus with the United States.\par \par \uc2 U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is seeking to push a crusade for fair trade and has ordered a study into the causes of U.S. trade deficits.\par \par \uc2 Thailand has submitted to the United States a report clarifying that the U.S. trade deficit with Thailand came from structural factors between their economies, not any unfair trade policies, Pimchanok Vonkhorporn, head of the Commerce Ministry's Trade Policy and Strategy Office, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 "The value of the deficit is not significantly high because it's only 1.5 percent of the total U.S. trade deficit," she said, adding that U.S. imports to Thailand were not high because it had no free trade agreements with Thailand.\par \par \uc2 Slow U.S. investment in Thailand and the impact of slow global growth on the Thai economy were among factors behind low Thai imports from the United States, Pimchanok said.\par \par \uc2 She said Thai exports to the United States and investment there also helped develop the U.S. economy and add U.S. jobs, while U.S. companies also used \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  as production bases.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not conducted any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies to gain an unfair advantage in trade and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  focused on stability in exchange rates, Pimchanok said.\par \par \uc2 Thailand had a trade surplus of about $18 billion with the United States last year, putting it 11th globally - well behind China's $347 billion surplus or nearby Vietnam's $32 billion.\par \par \uc2 The United States is a major market for Thai exports at a time the military government is struggling to revive growth in Southeast Asia's second's largest economy, which has lagged regional peers.\par \par \uc2 The ministry, in its report, drew attention to a 184-year relationship between Thailand and the United States, including in trade and investment, Pimchanok said. (Reporting by Orathai Sriring; Editing by Robert Birsel) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-5-11T13:26:43.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170511ed5b00ga7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
