\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Strong yuan supports Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 ; baht down on weaker growth\b0\par\par\uc2 717 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Februar 2016\par 06:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's yuan fixing biggest rise since Nov. 2\par     * Thai Q4 q/q growth below forecast, recovery still fragile\par     * Taiwan dollar near six-week high on inflows\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, Feb 15 (Reuters) - Most emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par rose on Monday after \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set its daily yuan\par guidance rate sharply firmer, easing concerns over risks of\par devaluation, and as regional stock markets clawed back some of\par their recent heavy losses.\par     Thailand's baht, however, fell as quarterly\par economic growth in the last three months of 2015 came in\par slightly weaker than expected. \par     The yuan jumped more than 1 percent to 6.4900 per\par dollar - its firmest this year - as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par set its daily midpoint 0.3 percent stronger before the market\par open. It was the official fixing's biggest rise since Nov. 2.\par  \par     The Taiwan dollar hit a near six-week high,\par tracking the renminbi's strength. The Malaysian ringgit \par rose after oil prices on Friday jumped more than 10 percent.\par     China's exports and imports in January fell more than\par expected, but emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  focused more on\par Beijing's stabilisation efforts for now, analysts said.\par  \par     In an interview over the weekend, PBOC Governor Zhou\par Xiaochuan said there was no basis for the yuan to keep falling,\par and China would keep it stable versus a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par while allowing greater volatility against the U.S. dollar.\par     "The weak export growth could add more speculation to CNY\par weakness," said Nordea Markets' senior analyst Amy Yuan Zhuang\par in Singapore, referring to the yuan.\par     "But I still believe that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Zhou Xiaochuan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's comments during\par the weekend and today's strong fixing rate helped alleviating\par some of the pressure on the CNY, at least temporarily. And so\par the same for Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ." \par     \par     BAHT\par     The baht slid as traders unwound bullish bets on growing\par caution over possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem\par its appreciation.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's economy grew 0.8 percent in October-December last\par year from the previous three months on a seasonally-adjusted\par basis, less than the 1.0 percent in July-September and the 0.9\par percent expected in a Reuters poll.\par     Government bond prices fell, adding to pressure on the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     \par     TAIWAN DOLLAR\par     The Taiwan dollar rose as much as 1.4 percent to 33.052 per\par the U.S. dollar, its strongest since Jan. 6.\par     The island's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  found further support from demand from\par foreign financial institutions as businesses reopened after a\par week-long break for the Lunar New Year holidays.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has not been spotted buying the U.S. Dollar\par to stem strength in the Taiwan dollar yet, traders said, but\par caution over \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remained.\par     \par     RINGGIT\par     The ringgit advanced 1 percent in thin trading as a rebound\par in crude prices eased concerns over Malaysia's falling oil and\par gas revenues.\par     The Malaysian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  found further support from demand\par against the neighbouring Singapore dollar.\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0450 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       113.84         113.27       -0.50\par   Sing dlr        1.3990         1.3974       -0.11\par   Taiwan dlr      33.052         33.520       +1.42\par   Korean won     1208.80        1211.70       +0.24\par   Baht             35.63          35.57       -0.17\par   Peso             47.53          47.51       -0.04\par   Rupiah        13410.00       13485.00       +0.56\par   Rupee            68.06          68.23       +0.25\par   Ringgit         4.1270         4.1700       +1.04\par   Yuan            6.4915         6.5755       +1.29\par  \par   Change so far in 2016\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       113.84         120.30       +5.67\par   Sing dlr        1.3990         1.4177       +1.34\par   Taiwan dlr      33.052         33.066       +0.04\par   Korean won     1208.80        1172.50       -3.00\par   Baht             35.63          36.00       +1.04\par   Peso             47.53          47.06       -0.99\par   Rupiah        13410.00       13785.00       +2.80\par   Rupee            68.06          66.15       -2.81\par   Ringgit         4.1270         4.2935       +4.03\par   Yuan            6.4915         6.4936       +0.03\par  \par  (Add