\par\par \uc2 Markets Main\par \b \uc2 Investors Sour on Southeast Asia's Growth Plans\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jake Maxwell Watts   \par \uc2 886 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2015\par 10:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top Energy Stories\par \uc2 DJTES\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Governments in Southeast Asia in the past several weeks have accelerated plans to boost growth and prop up markets amid worries about the spillover of China's slowdown.\par \par \uc2 But investors aren't convinced. In August and September combined, equity outflows from Malaysia, Indonesia and Thailand quickened at the fastest pace since the start of the year, according to data from EPFR Global.\par \par \uc2 In September alone, those countries announced programs that range from injecting cash into a fund used to buy stocks, to more actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. On Monday, Indonesia revealed facets of a new economic stimulus package, which will be detailed more fully in the near future.\par \par \uc2 Instead of reassuring investors, though, the moves left many skeptical about the effectiveness of stimulus.\par \par \uc2 "Boosting stock markets does not mean you boost domestic consumption and wealth," said Jalil Rasheed, investment director for Southeast Asian markets at \uc2 Invesco Ltd\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The plans follow spending announcements made earlier in the year, as regional economies continue to face gloomy Chinese economic data and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/investors-pull-about-40-billion-from-emerging-markets-in-current-quarter-1443553952?tesla=y"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prospect of higher interest rates in the U.S. }}}\par \par \uc2 One problem is that governments appear to be recycling old measures that didn't work well the first time.\par \par \uc2 Malaysia, for example, last month announced that it would give {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/malaysia-to-inject-more-money-into-economy-1442227006"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 20 billion ringgit ($4.6 billion) to a state-managed investment fund called ValueCap }}}\uc2 , which would buy some of the country's worst-hit stocks. Prime Minister \uc2 Najib Razak\uc2  also urged other quasi-independent state firms with overseas \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cash in those investments and reinvest in Malaysia.\par \par \uc2 The move recalls 2008 when Mr. Najib, then-deputy prime minister, announced government investment in Malaysian stocks through the same fund. The investments, worth about $1 billion, helped lift the local exchange out of a seven-month malaise but failed to lead to any lasting recovery.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Malaysia remains in a rut: Its economy grew 5% on year in the second quarter, the slowest pace since 2013. The country's main stock index has fallen around 7.5% since the beginning of the year and the ringgit has slumped nearly 30% against the U.S. dollar in that time.\par \par \uc2 Other investors are wary that governments may not follow through on promised economic reforms.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Indonesia's Finance Minister Bambang Brojonegoro announced plans to offer lower interest-rate loans to labor intensive small- and medium-size businesses as part of a future economic package. Officials didn't specify how big the stimulus spending would be or other planned programs. That follows moves last month to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/indonesia-relaxes-business-regulations-1441803218"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eliminate some 89 burdensome regulations }}}\uc2 , among other measures.\par \par \uc2 But "Indonesia has been a victim of this where policies are announced and revoked, during which time much damage has been done to stock market," said \uc2 Invesco\uc2 's Mr. Rasheed, whose firm manages $780 billion globally.\par \par \uc2 Foreign direct investment in Indonesia fell 2% in the first half of 2015 from a year earlier and economic growth has slipped to a six-year low. Stocks are down nearly 20% this year, Asia's worst performers, while the rupiah is down 18% against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 To support the rupiah, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced last week it would begin the unusual step of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards market, which is signaling that investors expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue weakening. That would be in addition what appears to be regular spot-market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where second-quarter growth stalled to 2.8% on year and stocks are down nearly 10% this year, the government in September announced a nearly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/how-thailand-plans-to-jump-start-its-economy-1443759023"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $4 billion economic stimulus package for small- and medium-size businesses }}}\uc2 , which includes interest-free loans and cash injections.\par \par \uc2 While the current government is eager to build more infrastructure, too, \uc2 Thailand's finance ministry\uc2  warned in early September that it may fall significantly short of disbursement of its investment budgets for the year.\par \par \uc2 "The Thai government has a horrible track record in terms of executing spending and this new government is confirming that trend," said Angelo Corbetta, head of Asia equity at \uc2 Pioneer Investments\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 To be sure, governments faced with stagnant economies and deteriorating financial markets may find they have few alternatives. Weak local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , hit by the prospect of rising interest rates in the U.S., limit central bankers' ability to cut rates for fear of prompting more outflows, leaving government policy as the only other tool capable of boosting a weak economy, said Mr. Corbetta.\par \par \uc2 The problem is that stimulus programs artificially boost asset prices and put governments on the hook for large amounts of spending. That only sets up a market for a bigger fall at a later date, when the support is withdrawn because government coffers are weaker, investors warn.\par \par \uc2 In fact, some say they would rather weather short-term losses than see governments stretch their finances on projects that lead to deeper economic stagnation down the road, such as Andy Seaman, chief investment officer for London-based Stratton Street Capital LLP, which manages $1.1 billion.\par \par \uc2 Short-term shocks are often followed with more rapid reform and better opportunities for investment, he said.\par \par \uc2 "I'm not sure it's sensible to try and revive markets."\par \par \uc2 I-Made Sentana contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jake Maxwell Watts at \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJTES11020151005eba500024\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
