\par\par \b \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 : Lower Oil Prices Mean No More BOJ Easing -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,640 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2015\par 08:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0714 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Lower oil prices will keep the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  from implementing further easing, even if lower oil prices also make its inflation target harder to achieve, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  economist Ryutaro Kono. The reason? The Japanese government has lowered pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as more easing would cause the yen to weaken further against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That would push imported energy prices up and squeeze consumer pocketbooks, he says. Mr. Kono expects that lower crude oil prices will help Japan's economy expand about 1.3% to 1.4% from a year earlier in real terms in the fiscal year that begins in April. (eleanor.warnock@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: TFD \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0706 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Thailand entered deflation in January for the first time in more than five years due to falling energy prices, with the headline consumer price index declining 0.4% from a year earlier. Get used to it, Nomura says. "We expect headline inflation to remain negative for several months, and core to fall with it," analyst Euben Paracuelles writes in a research note. Domestic demand is weak, while domestic spending fell 14.6% in November from a year earlier. That means inflation is here to stay for now, and the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  will likely cut rates by 25 basis points in March.(newley.purnell@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @Newley) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0705 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Idea Cellular\f28 \uc2 \f2  (532822.BY), India's third-biggest cellphone company by the number of users is seeking a bank license from \f28 \uc2 \f2 India's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which would allow it to pay out cash and accept deposits. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Idea Cellular\f28 \uc2 \f2 's application for a banking permit follows \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bharti Airtel\f28 \uc2 \f2  (532454.BY), India's biggest telecom company, which applied for its own permit last week. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Aditya Birla Nuvo\f28 \uc2 \f2  (500303.BY) will hold 51% stake in the new payments bank while the remainder will be held by Idea, the company said. Idea has about 150 million cellphone users on its system. Idea and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Aditya Birla Nuvo\f28 \uc2 \f2 , both part of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Aditya Birla Group\f28 \uc2 \f2 , did not disclose the financial details of the deal. (krishna.jai@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @jaikrishna) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0653 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bombay Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 's banking index is the worst performing among 12 sectoral indices, trading down 1.1% after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  kept its repurchase rate unchanged at 7.75%. "Investors were hoping that there would be a rate cut but are disappointed," says Rakesh Shinde, a research analyst covering banks at Mumbai-broker Bonanza Portfolio Pvt. Ltd. The RBI had, last month, unexpectedly cut its policy rate for the first time in nearly two years to support sluggish economic growth. A rate cut would have revived credit growth which was down for the past one year, says Shinde. Also, lower rates would have helped borrowers pay back as non-performing assets are on the rise, he says. The benchmark \f28 \uc2 \f2 S&P\f28 \uc2 \f2  BSE Sensex is trading down 0.6%. (kenan.machado@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0650 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Though India won't be immune to global market volatility that may come with the end of quantitative easing, it is in a much better position to handle the situation, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Raghuram Rajan\f28 \uc2 \f2 , governor of the Reserve Bank of India. "We have made important strides on at least three out of four macro variables," says Rajan. He notes that India's economic growth is rising, and inflation and the current-account deficit have fallen. Now, the government needs to announce a credible plan later this month to shrink its fiscal deficit, he says.(shefali.anand@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0638 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/THB appears supportive on the daily chart--hovering above its 200-day moving average at 32.45--but that may be due to suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , a trader says. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may have stepped in to suppress volatility after the baht gained sharply versus the U.S. dollar on Monday, creating a bearish technical cue that generated spillover downward momentum in USD/THB Tuesday. The dollar fell as low as 32.48 from its Monday close of 32.57 before rebounding to 32.50. Below the 200-day moving average at 32.45 lies plenty of stop-loss orders, the trader said, and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to dampen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fluctuation may be crucial in preventing another sharp spike in the baht when those stop-loss orders are triggered. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: TFD \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0634 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  tweaks rules for foreign investors buying Indian bonds, saying that in future they will be able to buy only company bonds that have a minimum residual maturity of three years. However, foreign investors will not have a lock-in and will be able to sell these bonds at any time. "We'd like to nudge people toward the longer end," says RBI Governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Raghuram Rajan\f28 \uc2 \f2  at a press conference Tuesday. (shefali.anand@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0628 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] India may not go for more big stake sales in state-run companies in the current financial year through March, says Jagannadham Thunuguntla, head of fundamental research at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Karvy Stock Broking\f28 \uc2 \f2  after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Life Insurance Corp. of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  revealed it made a large cash injection in recently concluded 10% share sale of government's equity in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Coal India Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (533278.BY). "Such big stake sales are one-off events. I don't see any more big stake sale happening in near future," he told the Wall Street Journal. "This also reaffirms the point that smaller share sales with good prices find more takers." On Friday, he had said there would be no dearth of investors for government stake sales this fiscal. He now says government may look at sale of smaller equity holding in government and private companies.(saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; @journosaurabh) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0628 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Indonesian government's rupiah bond yields may not fall much further from the current level as bonds are already overvalued following a net $3.1 billion inflows last month, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mandiri Sekuritas\f28 \uc2 \f2 ' chief debt market analyst Handy Yunianto. "In January 2015, Indonesian government's local-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bonds recorded the highest return among Asian government's local-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bonds," Yunianto says, adding that the return of the Indonesian government's rupiah bonds was 6.36% in January compared with the average 1.5% return booked by Asia's local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign bonds. He says the large foreign inflows last month were likely due to reallocation of some funds from Russian markets to Indonesia as well as from some eurozone nations, thanks to liquidity created by monetary easing. The inflows boosted foreign holding of rupiah sovereign bonds to a record 500 trillion rupiah--or 40% of the total outstanding amount--as of January 30, government data show. Finance Minister Bambang Brojonegoro said to The Wall Street Journal in December that the government would seek to cut the volume of government debt held by foreign funds, to insulate against the risk of large-scale capital flight. The government will do this by increasing the participation of local investors in the domestic debt market. (i-made.sentana@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0621 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] South Korea's weak export and inflation data, driven by cheap oil, call for further policy-rate cuts to spur growth, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  economist Ronald Man. "Weak economic activity in Korea supports our view that monetary policy remains biased towards more easing," Mr. Man says. He says he expects \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2  to cut the base rate by 0.25 percentage point to 1.75% in the first quarter and another 0.25 percentage point later in the year. Inflation remained tepid with data out Tuesday showing Korea's consumer-price index rose 0.8% on the year in January after gaining at the same pace in December. Trade data out Sunday showed Korea's exports in January fell 0.4% from a year earlier after a revised 3.6% gain in December. Lower crude prices have cut into the value of Korea's overseas shipments of oil-refinery and petrochemical products while keeping energy imports and inflation subdued. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: FLS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Violeta Todorova is the correct name of a senior technical analyst at Morgans in Brisbane. "Aussie Shares Could Surge to 6000 in 2H15: Technical Analyst -- Market Talk," at 0521 GMT, misstated her name as Natalia Todorova.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0612 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  keeps an underweight rating on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Coal India Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 .(533278.BY), with an unchanged target price of INR325, after the Indian government raised $3.6 billion from a 10% stake sale in the world's biggest coal producer. "With the large stake sale behind us, we believe investor focus would now shift to the quantum of dividend (possible announcement with 3Q results next week); potential price hikes after coal block auctions are over; and more clarity on non-coal mine investment," it says. "While we see limited absolute downside, we expect \f28 \uc2 \f2 Coal India\f28 \uc2 \f2  to be a relative underperformer and maintain our underweight rating." It expects a dividend of INR16 a share after the stake sale, below the dividend of INR29 a share in FY14. Last year, the government, the biggest shareholder in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Coal India\f28 \uc2 \f2 , pushed the company for a big dividend payout as the planned stake sale couldn't go through amid opposition from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Coal India\f28 \uc2 \f2 's unions. This year, as the government has already raised a record $3.6 billion via the stake sale, a big dividend looks unlikely. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; @journosaurabh) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: ALP \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 03, 2015 02:14 ET (07:14 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 