\par\par \b \uc2 Euro To Suffer From Weaker Euro-Zone Inflows, Says Nomura -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,377 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 2014\par 14:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1208 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Weaker inflows into euro-zone equities are likely to translate into a weaker euro, says Jens Nordvig at Nomura. "This is important, since strong portfolio flows have consistently supported the euro over the last 1-2 years," he says, adding that the ECB is clearly going to remain easy for very long. This is not reflected yet in rate differentials. "Hence, the euro's resilience in crosses has a lot to do with global rates still being under downward pressure," he says. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com; @chiaraalbanese)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1151 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--Credit market valuations are becoming less and less attractive for investors, says Giordano Lombardo, chief investment officer at Pioneer Investments. "Spread compression has continued across the whole ratings spectrum. The hunt for yield induced by central banks' zero interest rate policies has pushed the share of credit assets in household portfolios to unprecedented levels," he says. Meanwhile, liquidity in the trading market is fading. Even if investment-grade and high-yield credit retain some appeal in comparison with core government bonds, the upside potential is very limited. "Credit markets need to be handled carefully," he says. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese)\par \par \uc2 1142 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--Gilts remain in the red after Tuesday's inflation data surprised to the upside (June CPI 1.9% y/y vs 1.6% expectations). This has pushed forward rate hike expectations, according to analysts at ING, who now see the first move in November. "This would also remove political concerns of waiting until early next year when election campaigning is in full swing and should keep sterling supported," says ING. The September gilt contract is 0.24 lower at 110.41 and the 10-year yields 2.631%. (nick.cawley@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1139 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Thailand isn't ready to raise rates, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says. "For the time being it is deemed appropriate for Thailand to adopt a relaxed monetary policy," \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  Gov. Prasarn Trairatvorakul says. "However, monetary policy may be adjusted...if the situation calls for it in the future." The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has cut its benchmark interest rate twice to 2% to shore up the economy since political unrest in November. (nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1124 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] A bond backed by loans to Bulgaria's Corporate Commercial Bank, which last week had its banking licence revoked by the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has halved in price in the last month, according to a trader. The $150 million securitized bond--Northern Lights Bulgaria BV--which was arranged by Russian bank VTB is being quoted at a cash price of around 45%-55% of face value, the trader says. The bond had been quoted at a whisker below face value as recently as June 18. "Given the size of the deal and VTB as a sole lead manager, I don't imagine there is much of this trading or anyone who is willing to take the other side given a bank wind up in Bulgaria is relatively unprecedented," the trader says. (ben.edwards@wsj.com @debtnerd)\par \par \uc2 7:17 EDT - The only focus of the \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this week's meeting will be attempting to fuel the country's tepid economic activity despite inflationary pressures, says Luciano Rostagno at \uc2 Mizuho Bank\uc2 . Like others, it expects interest rates standing pat as part of tomorrow's policy statement. Brazil's annual inflation in June surpassed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tolerance band of up to 6.5%, but some have predicted a below-zero number for 2Q GDP. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1108 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  vows to maintain the stability of the Thai baht following calls from the military government to rein in the value of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "So far, the baht movement has not caused any problem to Thailand's trade or investment," says Prasarn Trairatvorakul, governor of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "The BOT will stand ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the value of the baht weakens or strengthens too much and too fast in order to prevent any impact on local economy." Since the beginning of 2014, the baht has strengthened nearly 2% against the dollar, according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:03 EDT - \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's (\uc2 JPM\uc2 ) bottom-line results haven't been impressive of late, in part on legal costs impacting earnings. That's kept shares going largely sideways the past year. The big bank's 2Q results are likely to please at least some with EPS falling much less than Wall Street anticipated as revenue dropped just 2%, not 8% as was the consensus view. "Core" loans rose 8% from a year earlier amid "record" business-banking loan originations while deposits climbed. CEO \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  adds there were "encouraging signs" of increased business in numerous parts of \uc2 JPM\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the quarter came to a close. A real 2H economic pickup perhaps? Say it isn't so. Shares, down 1.9% since the 1Q report, rise 1.5% premarket to $57.13. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 1103 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] While the Aussie dollar is not having much luck on the topside, the U.K. clearer looks for buying interest in $0.9320 area to stem any further weakness. On the upside, the bank is looking for a return to the recent high of $0.9460 and on a break here, $0.9500. AUD now trades at $0.9374. (gary.stride@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1057 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--\uc2 Banco Espirito Santo\uc2 's (BES.LB) subordinated bond, which carries a coupon of 7.125% and is due in November 2018, continues to tumble on fears that holders of that paper could suffer in the wake of financial trouble at the broader group. Yields on the bonds have risen by 3.63 percentage points since Monday's close and the cost of insuring the bank's debt against default has risen by 56 basis points to 503 basis points. It now costs an average of $503,000 a year to insure $10 million of BES debt for five years. (josie.cox@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1054 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Indonesia's political situation will likely remain peaceful after the release later this month of the official results from the July 9 presidential election, says Ikrar Nusa Bhakti, political analyst with the state-controlled think tank Indonesian Institute of Science. "Historically, Indonesian presidential and legislative elections had never triggered any violence or anarchy," Mr. Bhakti says. He adds the military and police are "more prepared" to safeguard this election. Former Gen. Prabowo Subianto has said he believes he is leading the official vote count despite independent polling suggesting his rival, Jakarta Gov. Joko Widodo, is ahead. Final vote totals are scheduled to be unveiled July 22. (i-made.sentana@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1030 GMT The July 7 reaction low at 0.7916 in EUR/GBP has moved ominously back into the immediate picture, following Tuesday's weakness. Resistance at 0.7980 has remained resolute, and the downward breach of 0.7937 has consigned Monday's 0.7980 high to bull failure status, signalling risk for a full reversal back to the 0.7916 reaction low. A bear flag continuation pattern has been completed on the daily chart, and a break below 0.7916 would indicate additional downside scope to 0.7880 is achievable. EUR/GBP is at 0.7932. -By Francis Bray, \uc2 Dow Jones Chief Technical\uc2  Analyst For Europe.(francis.bray@dowjones.com) (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 . The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 15, 2014 08:08 ET (12:08 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140715ea7f001l7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
