\par\par \b \uc2 Thai martial law hits baht, stocks but losses seen limited for now\b0\par\par\uc2 526 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 2014\par 06:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Thai army declares martial law, but says not a coup               \par \par \uc2 * Baht pares losses; \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected               \par \par \uc2 * Benchmark stock index slides 1 percent               \par \par \uc2 * Analysts say 'bad news' priced in  By Jongwoo Cheon and Satawasin Staporncharnchai               \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE/BANGKOK, May 20 (Reuters) - Thai stocks and the baht \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slid on Tuesday after the army imposed martial law in a bid to restore stability after months of protests left the country without a properly functioning government.               \par \par \uc2 But analysts did not expect a significant exodus of foreign funds unless violence escalates and threatens further damage to Southeast Asia's second-largest economy.               \par \par \uc2 The army denied that it was staging a military coup and a caretaker government remained in office.               \par \par \uc2 The baht fell as much as 0.5 percent to 32.62 per dollar in early trade. It later pared most of those losses and was quoted at 32.48 as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said.               \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's main share index lost around 1 percent, while the five- and 10-year government bond yields   rose.               \par \par \uc2 "The near term bias is for further USD/THB strength, with 32.85/95 some initial upside targets to think about," said Jonathan Cavenagh, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 Westpac\uc2  in Singapore, referring to the dollar/baht pair.               \par \par \uc2 The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to remain an underperformer against the likes of the Malaysian ringgit and the Philippine peso, Cavenagh said.               \par \par \uc2 The economy shrank more than expected in the first quarter as anti-government protests dragged on, adding to concerns the country is sliding into recession.               \par \par \uc2 The Thai bond market has seen 51.1 billion baht ($1.6 billion) of outflows so far this year, even though foreigners have bought a combined net 19.6 billion baht in bonds, according to \uc2 Thai Bond Market Association\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 In the stock market, foreign investors have sold a combined net 7.5 billion baht so far this year, according to exchange data.               \par \par \uc2 Yet, the main index is still up more than 7 percent so far this year.               \par \par \uc2 Analysts said some investors may be looking to buy Thai assets on dips.               \par \par \uc2 Thailand's government bonds have been thriving on gloomy economic and political headlines as investors line up to buy baht-oriented debt.               \par \par \uc2 "We expect interest rates will remain low, especially as the strengthening current account insulates THB from external pressures," \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  said in a research note, referring to the baht.               \par \par \uc2 "We continue to advise clients against being short THB. By minimizing violence, martial law could limit the impact on the critical tourism sector. The current account improvement may then outstrip potential capital outflows."               \par \par \uc2 Thailand reported a combined $8.2 billion current account surplus in the first three months of the year.               \par \par \uc2 Thai assets have already priced in the prolonged political unrest, analysts said.               \par \par \uc2 Last year, foreigners dumped a net 194.7 billion baht in Thai stocks and bond market suffered 942.0 billion baht ofoutflows.               \par \par \uc2 "Even if the bias is for equities to weaken we could still see bargain hunters support the market," \uc2 Westpac\uc2 's Jonathan said.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-5-20T05:59:47.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140520ea5k0046b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
