\par\par \b \uc2 Thai Stocks Fall After Martial Law Declared\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jake Maxwell Watts   \par \uc2 543 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 2014\par 07:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Thai stocks fell Tuesday after the army stepped in following six months of political unrest by declaring martial law and placing soldiers onto the streets of Bangkok.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark SET index fell 1.2% with the exchange earlier forced to temporarily close its doors because troops were positioned outside. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  initially fell, too, on worries the early-morning move by the head of the armed services might signal a potential coup, but it later climbed on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Is this doing \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  any favors? No, in the eyes of the world, it looks like they can't sort themselves out and their brand is definitely taking a beating," said Jalil Rasheed, investment director at Invesco, which holds assets in Thailand and is still optimistic about the long-term outlook.\par \par \uc2 "I believe the military has stepped in to expedite the process of facilitating a properly elected government and won't stay in power indefinitely," Mr. Rasheed added.\par \par \uc2 The protests in Thailand toppled Prime Minister Yingluck Shinawatra earlier this month, with the political divide characterized by mass street rallies and occasionally fatal attacks between the two main groups of protesters.\par \par \uc2 The imposition of martial law follows data Monday that showed the economy contracted in the first quarter, which analysts say could lead to a recession this year. Analysts are predicting a further cut to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's core interest rate at its June meeting.\par \par \uc2 "Where financial markets go from here depends on the likelihood of blood being spilled," said ING economist Tim Condon. "Thai-on-Thai violence would be bad for Thai financial assets. The violence would have to be truly awful for financial assets outside Thailand to be affected," he added.\par \par \uc2 The losses in Thai stocks were evenly spread across the board early Tuesday. \uc2 Kasikornbank PCL\uc2  lost 1.2%, while telecommunications company \uc2 Advanced Info Service PCL\uc2  lost 1.3%.\par \par \uc2 Despite increased volatility, however, Thailand's financial markets have largely remained resilient in the face of the political tension. The baht has strengthened 0.5% against the U.S. dollar year-to-date, in line with most of its regional peers, and traded Tuesday at 32.49 per dollar, 0.4% stronger than Monday's close. The SET gained 7.6% in the January-March period, marking the third-best performance among Asian stock markets.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts hope that martial law could help prompt a resolution to the political deadlock. "The imposition of martial law is not, in itself, negative for Thailand's ratings, although clearly we are keeping the situation under close review," said Andrew Colquhoun, head of Asia-Pacific sovereign ratings at \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He said that the most important factors for ratings are whether Thailand can avoid further disorder and install a government capable of passing a budget to put back on track policies that have been derailed by the unrest. "The return of a functioning government and a lowering of the political temperature would help to restore confidence more broadly and pull the economy out of its tailspin," he said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jake Maxwell Watts at jake.watts@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 20, 2014 01:50 ET (05:50 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140520ea5k000hp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
