\par\par \b \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  Meeting Begins as Officials Feel Upbeat on Capex -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,391 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 2014\par 04:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0230 GMT [Dow Jones] With no policy change expected at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's latest two-day monetary policy meeting starting Tuesday, focus rests on BOJ's views on the economy roughly two months since the sales tax rate was raised. Following a recent string of strong data, BOJ may upgrade its view on capital investment. That could further reduce the chance of additional easing steps near-term, as strength in capex would theoretically help offset some of the weakness in consumption seen in the wake of the tax hike. But while recent economic data may be encouraging, sluggish Tokyo stock markets and the recovering yen are now casting a shadow over Japan's economic outlook. Investors will look to scrutinize Mr. Kuroda's remarks (a press conference likely at 0630 GMT Wednesday) to see whether, and how strongly, he is concerned about them. (takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0226 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro/yen cross is displaying a buoyant tone Tuesday as Asian stocks traded mostly higher (Japan's Nikkei Stock Average was recently up 0.63% at 14,095.36; Shanghai Composite Index up 0.38% at 2,012.82). Further upside in spot EUR/JPY may encounter resistance at 139.44 and 139.90. Spot EUR/JPY was recently at 139.24 compared with 139.15 in late New York trade Monday. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0224 GMT [Dow Jones] Suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lifted the baht off its intraday low of 32.65 to its current 32.52 versus the U.S. dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely sold U.S. dollars from 32.65 down to as low as 32.60 before the market took the cue and liquidated speculative long-USD positions, said a couple of traders based in Singapore. The Thai baht has clawed back from sharp losses sustained early Tuesday, when the market reacted to news of military law declared in Thailand. At its Tuesday trough, the baht fell 0.6% versus its Monday close. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0216 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares open up on bargain hunting, after the country's securities regulator said late Monday that there would be around 100 new listings from June till the year end, easing some concerns about a share supply glut. The benchmark Shanghai Composite Index is up 0.4% at 2013.94, tipped to face resistance at 2050 level. "There were previous expectations that there would be 30 new listings every month, but the announcement yesterday cut that by around half," says Tang Yonggang, an analyst at Hongyuan Securities. "This is a breather for the market, but it's likely to be shortlived. The CSRC didn't mention how much money these firms plan to raise, and a big IPO could still put pressure on liquidity," he says. Among actives, \uc2 Shanghai Pudong Development Bank\uc2  (600000.SH) is up 0.4% at CNY9.56 and \uc2 Citic Securities\uc2  (600030.SH) is 1.0% higher at CNY11.17. The Shenzhen Composite Index is up 1.1% at 1025.14.\par \par \uc2 (esther.fung@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] China's stock investors may take a breather on the securities regulator's comments that about 100 companies will be listed on the nation's stock market this year. In 2012, the year before a 14-month moratorium was imposed on the IPO market, China allowed 154 companies to conduct IPOs, which raised 103.4 billion yuan ($16.6 billion) in total. Fewer IPOs this year may help soothe investor jitters over massive new-share supply and arrest a further slide in the stock market, says \uc2 Sinolink Securities\uc2  analyst Huang Cendong. Investors were previously expecting 300 to 400 companies to be listed, Huang adds. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is little changed on thin volume amid a dearth of fresh trading cues, says a local trader. The pair is at 30.142, slightly higher than 30.130 traded before the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday, which lifted it to 30.185 at the close of onshore trading session, traders say. A trader says the USD/TWD is somewhat muted about Thailand's political conflicts which pushed the greenback higher against most regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the pair may stay in a consolidation mode between 30.100 and 30.150 for the session. Continued foreign fund inflows to local stocks still help support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; the Taiex is up 0.3%. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0211 GMT [Dow Jones] Despite a slight rise to 43.74 from Monday's close of 43.63, USD/PHP continues to look bearish on the daily chart as long as it stays below 43.75 and thereby inside the Bollinger downtrend channel. The immediate downside target for USD/PHP is the base of the channel at 43.34. The improved outlook for the Philippines economy--acknowledged by S&P's credit rating upgrade--due to the actions of a proactive \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep the peso in demand. On the other side of the equation, the U.S. dollar remains soggy due to depressed \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  bond yields. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0211 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD eased back on positioning ahead of the RBA minutes and remains lower after the event, says Stuart Ive, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at OM Financial. "It was mainly people positioning themselves in case there was anything too different in the statement," he says. However, "there are no surprises in it," with \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reiterating it expects interest rates will likely remain at record low levels for some time yet. The pair is at 0.8616 versus 0.8637 earlier in the session. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 0209 GMT [Dow Jones] The Australian dollar slipped to a two-week low of 94.34 from 94.50 against the yen after minutes of the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2 's May 6 policy meeting indicated that low rates are "likely appropriate for some time." But AUD/JPY losses were limited by rising Tokyo stocks (Japan's Nikkei Stock Average was recently up 0.70% at 14,104.54). Further downside in the Aussie/yen cross may encounter support at 94.22 (May 5 reaction low). Spot AUD/JPY was recently at 94.47 compared with 94.66 in late New York trade Monday. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [Dow Jones] Analysts tip BMD crude palm oil to open flat Tuesday, as traders await cues from export data due to be released later in the day. A Kuala Lumpur-based trader tips CPO to trade between MYR2,530-MYR2,535/ton. "There are rumors that the Malaysian export data will be quite supportive, at about 880,000 tons" for the period May 1-20, he says. Benchmark August BMD CPO on Monday closed down 1.7% at MYR2,536/ton.\par \par \uc2 (isabella.steger@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [Dow Jones] While Japanese car brands in China are expected to perform well this year, longer term the picture is "complicated," Bernstein Research said. "Nationalistic feelings are an impediment. Premium brands will struggle," analyst Max Warburton writes in a research note. Japanese brands and their Chinese joint venture partners will deliver good growth this year, he says. "But long-term, this will be a German market." \uc2 Volkswagen AG\uc2  and \uc2 Brilliance China Automotive Holdings\uc2  look "well positioned." (colum.murphy@wsj.com \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @Colum _M)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT [Dow Jones] Tocom RSS3 rubber futures soften on Tuesday following a rally on Monday in Shanghai and Tokyo. Chiaki Furui, chief executive of commodities brokerage Agrow Enterprise in Bangkok says traders are "confused" because there are "so many factors" in the market. Some traders may have been short-covering following rumors that sales of rubber from Vietnam to China may be affected by tensions between the two countries, he says. The Thai army declared martial law on Tuesday, further complicating the picture for rubber. Benchmark October rubber trades Y1.1 lower at Y205.5/kg.(isabella.steger@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 19, 2014 22:30 ET (02:30 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140520ea5k0009e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
