\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ Blog:Would Thailand Opt for an Exchange-Rate Target?\b0\par\par\uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juli 2013\par 08:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's Real Time Economics blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/economics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/economics}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 By Michael S. Arnold\par \par \uc2 Hints that the Federal Reserve would start winding down its massive bond-buying program have sent shockwaves through Thai markets in recent months, sparking an outflow of funds that sent local stocks, bonds and the baht \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunging.\par \par \uc2 It also sparked some unusual policy proposals -- including a call for Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop targeting inflation and focus instead on maintaining a stable exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  uses interest rates to keep core prices within a desired range, currently set at 0.5%-3.0%. But Supavud Saicheua, executive director and head of research at Bangkok-based brokerage Phatra Securities Plc, says a monetary policy regime focused on the exchange rate would be far more effective for a relatively small, trade-dependent economy.\par \par \uc2 "They're pointing their gun in the wrong place," Mr. Saicheua said of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "It's ironic that they're so explicit about what they're trying to do with inflation but the source of instability is not inflation, it's the exchange rate. And Thailand has no policy framework to address that."\par \par \uc2 Like other developing economies, Thailand saw its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and markets soar earlier this year as money unleashed by massive quantitative easing in the United States and Japan flowed into emerging markets. The baht was Asia's best performer for much of the year, gaining more than 7% against the U.S. dollar to a multi-year high of 28.54 on April 22 -- much to the chagrin of Thai exporters.\par \par \uc2 Then the U.S. Federal Reserve began talking of "tapering" its quantitative easing program. Within weeks the baht had given up all the year's gains and more, while Thailand's benchmark stock index fell by 18% from peak to trough. Companies that had been complaining about a strong baht now were complaining that it was too weak.\par \par \uc2 Regardless, the idea of targeting the exchange rate is likely to be a hard sell at the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2 . Gov. Prasarn Traivoratkul told the Journal that the current framework is the most transparent, making it easier to manage inflation expectations and avoid damaging spirals in which price increases and expectations of future increases feed off each other.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, "exchange-rate targeting is the least transparent regime," he said. "Nobody can spell out what exchange rate they want because the market can front-run, the market can manipulate and attack, and sometimes you cannot defend yourself."\par \par \uc2 That's precisely what happened in 1997, when Thailand had a fixed exchange rate. Speculators who calculated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't have enough reserves to protect the baht from sustained attack ultimately forced a devaluation of more than 50% that broke the back of Thailand's economy and forced the government into the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's austere embrace. The financial crisis spread from Bangkok to the rest of Asia, wreaking havoc across the continent.\par \par \uc2 Today most Asian central banks have inflation-targeting regimes to one degree or another, though many -- including the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  during periods of unusual volatility.\par \par \uc2 On one point, at least, Messrs. Prasarn and Saicheua agree: The specter of 1997 makes it highly unlikely the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  would ever switch back to an exchange-rate target.\par \par \uc2 That option "has been totally discredited" by the events of 1997, Mr. Saicheua said. "Inflation targeting is the nice guy with a clean image and good reputation. Exchange-rate targeting just creates a sense of horror whenever you talk about it." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 23, 2013 02:14 ET (06:14 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130723e97n000m7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
