\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Thai c.bank not ruling out strong measures to curb robust baht\b0\par\par\uc2 616 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 2013\par 04:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Baht has risen nearly 6 pct vs dollar this year\par \par \uc2  * BOT satisfied with its approach on exchange rate management\par \par \uc2  * Finance minister wants rates cut to hold \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down \par \par \uc2 (Adds background, baht moves)\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, April 17 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  has not ruled out strong measures to halt the baht's rise but its monetary policy committee (MPC) believes the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has the means to address the problem if needed, according to minutes of its April meeting.\par \par \uc2  "The MPC discussed the potential drivers of recent capital inflow and baht appreciation, including the misperception by some market participants that the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  had ruled out strong policy responses," the minutes published on Wednesday said.\par \par \uc2  "The MPC, however, was satisfied that the BOT's current exchange rate management framework already entailed systematic steps of policy responses, with specific measures ready to be implemented if and when warranted by the circumstances."\par \par \uc2  At the meeting on April 3, the monetary policy committee voted to leave the policy rate at 2.75 percent, ignoring government calls for a cut to deter "hot money" flows and reiterating it was concerned about credit growth and potential asset bubbles. \par \par \uc2  The baht has risen almost 6 percent against the dollar this year, by some distance the biggest rise among Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  The export industry has expressed concern about the baht's rise and Finance Minister Kittirat Na Ranong has repeatedly called for interest rates to be cut to diminish the allure of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  The \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  has appeared relaxed, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market very little, if at all, and has noted that the baht was merely playing catch-up with appreciation in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the Thai economy was suffering from severe flooding and its aftermath in 2011 and 2012.\par \par \uc2  However, on April 9 Governor Prasarn Trairatvorakul said the baht had been rising too quickly and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was concerned. \par \par \uc2  He said it would look at the baht again after Songkran, the Thai New Year, on April 13-16. Thai financial markets were closed for that holiday on Monday and Tuesday.\par \par \uc2  The baht is trading around 16-year highs. It hit 28.91 per dollar in early trade on Wednesday, up from levels around 28.98 on Friday before the long holiday, and then ticked down immediately after the minutes were released.\par \par \uc2  At 0300 GMT, it was quoted at 28.95/98 per dollar.\par \par \uc2  Most economists expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to leave its policy rate at 2.75 percent for the rest of the year. A small minority  believes a cut is possible if the global economy struggles and exports don't pick up, but most expect the next move to be an increase.\par \par \uc2  In the minutes, the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  repeated its concern about risks to financial stability centred on household credit growth and "incipient signs of speculation" in the property market.\par \par \uc2  "Inflationary pressure may edge up going forward due to supply constraints, higher labour costs and greater cost pass-through, given entrepreneurs' diminishing ability to hold down prices," it added.\par \par \uc2  It has been less worried about inflation than some private economists and on April 12 trimmed its forecast for 2013 to 2.7 percent from 2.8 percent.\par \par \uc2  It cut its forecast for core inflation, which excludes energy and fresh food prices, to 1.6 percent from 1.7 percent. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sets interest rates with the aim of keeping core inflation in a 0.5 to 3.0 percent range. \par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Alan Raybould; Editing by Shri Navaratnam)\par \par \uc2 THAILAND-ECONOMY/MINUTES  (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130417e94h0002g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
