\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Thai baht hits 16-yr high, sparking  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  talk\b0\par\par\uc2 By Orathai Sriring and Jongwoo Cheon   \par \uc2 975 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2013\par 05:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and analysts comments)\par \par \uc2 BANGKOK/SINGAPORE, March 20 (Reuters) - The Thai baht hit its highest level on Wednesday since the Asian financial crisis in 1997 and economists said the resilient economy would continue to attract foreign money, though some cautioned the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to deter speculators.\par \par \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Prasarn Trairatvorakul, who has argued until now that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise was in line with economic fundamentals and with the trend in other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said on Wednesday it was "a bit too much and fast".\par \par \uc2 The baht, gaining on a day when Cypriot rejection of the terms for a European bail-out hurt other risky assets, rose as much as 0.7 percent to 29.13 per dollar and stood at 29.15 in late trade.\par \par \uc2 It has risen around 5 percent so far this year, making it Asia's best performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Technically, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been seen as excessively overbought, with the dollar/baht's 14-day relative strength index at 16.6, far below the 30-threshold, but many market watchers predict further modest gains.\par \par \uc2 "We are looking at 29 baht per dollar by year-end. Foreign inflows should continue to support it in the short term," said Somchai Amornthum, a senior vice-president at Krung Thai Asset Management.\par \par \uc2 "The rise should not be tackled by interest rates because it will have a wide impact and we also have to be careful about bubbles ... What they can do is to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, both verbally and with direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he added.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Kittirat Na Ranong has put pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut interest rates to deter foreign inflows but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has resisted.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, after a meeting with the governor, Kittirat ruled out capital controls.\par \par \uc2 "We will not issue any special measure to deal with the strong baht," he told reporters, saying such moves could cause panic in the markets and undermine investor confidence. Capital controls in 2006 caused a plunge in the bourse.\par \par \uc2 The government is worried that a strong baht will hurt exports, which generally drive economic growth in Thailand.\par \par \uc2 But Kirida Bhaopichitr, country economist for Thailand at the \uc2 World Bank\uc2 , told a seminar on Wednesday there was no strong correlation between the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and exports.\par \par \uc2 "Actually, Thai exports rely a lot more on global demand ... You can see that when the exchange rate appreciates... all Thai exports also increase. But for 2009, for example, although our exchange rate depreciated, exports fell," she said.\par \par \uc2 The economy grew 6.4 percent in 2012 and the National Economic and Social Development Board (NESDB), which compiles the data, forecasts 4.5 to 5.5 percent growth for this year.\par \par \uc2 DRIVEN BY FUNDAMENTALS\par \par \uc2 Gundy Cahyadi, an economist with OCBC in Singapore, also remained positive on the baht, given the potential in the economy.\par \par \uc2 "The key test will be to see if it can break the 29.00 mark - it's definitely not to be ruled out," he said.\par \par \uc2 "Certainly, the issue of speculative inflows needs to be monitored, because these tend to be destabilising. But we agree with the assessment of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that the baht this year has been driven by fundamentals."\par \par \uc2 Enrico Tanuwidjaja, an economist with \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2 , said he was sticking to the end-2013 forecast he made in October of 29 baht per dollar, citing "strong domestic demand and a good investment story," and adding Thailand has been a relatively underweighted market for quite a long time.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance, he said: "I guess it's still within their comfort zone ... It's in line with regionals in terms of catching up. You can plot 2012 gains versus current levels. It's playing catch-up."\par \par \uc2 Thailand was devastated by floods in late 2011 and manufacturing was slow to recover in 2012. The baht rose just over 3 percent last year, whereas the Philippine peso rose 6.8 percent and the South Korean won 7.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 Traders said offshore funds bought the baht ahead of a bond sale on Wednesday at which the government raised 20 billion baht ($682 million).\par \par \uc2 A 6.23-year bond yielded 3.2835 percent against 3.3877 percent for a similar bond on Jan. 30.\par \par \uc2 Thai bond markets also saw inflows of about $340 million on Monday and Tuesday, according to traders. That followed a record single-day inflow of 28 billion baht ($954 million) on March 12.\par \par \uc2 Foreign flows into the stock market have been patchy since end-January but may be revived by what could be Thailand's biggest IPO, when Bangkok SkyTrain operator \uc2 BTS Group Holdings\uc2  Pcl raises up to $2.1 billion by listing an infrastructure fund next month.\par \par \uc2 Thailand's main stock index has surged nearly 13 percent so far this year.\par \par \uc2 Infrastructure plans are also attracting inflows. On Tuesday the cabinet approved a bill to borrow 2 trillion baht to fund infrastructure projects that are expected to fuel economic growth over the next seven years.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister \uc2 Yingluck Shinawatra\uc2  said some funding might be from overseas but Kittirat said loans would be found at home.\par \par \uc2 The baht also is benefiting from Tokyo's expansionary policies, with Japanese manufacturers stepping up investment in Thailand.\par \par \uc2 In January, \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2  said it would invest 12 billion baht, while \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  said in February it would spend more than 2 billion baht on a new assembly plant.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 29.3350 Thai baht)\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Saikat Chatterjee in Hong Kong, plus Viparat Jantraprap, Paul Carsten and Pairat Temphairojana in Bangkok; Writing by Alan Raybould; Editing by Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-THAILAND-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 / (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | ABX | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130320e93k0004o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
