\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-S.Korea, Thailand warn over fallout from easy money,  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Se Young Lee and Kitiphong Thaichareon   \par \uc2 976 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2013\par 11:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  SEOUL/BANGKOK, Jan 30 (Reuters) - South Korea warned on Wednesday it might consider a tax on financial transactions and Thailand said it was worried its strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would hurt exporters as moves by advanced economies to flood markets with easy money increasingly spill over into other countries.\par \par \uc2  Government officials in two of the four Asian tiger economies, which fell victim to massive outflows of speculative money in the late 1990s, expressed concerns over the negative impacts of super-loose monetary policy just two weeks before a \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  finance chiefs' meeting in Moscow.\par \par \uc2  Policymakers in advanced countries, particularly Japan and the United States, have been acting aggressively to print more money and reflate their economies since the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2  This has had the effect of weakening their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  while strengthening those of many other countries from South Korea to Mexico, making their exports less competitive, roiling their financial markets and threatening to feed destabilising asset bubbles.\par \par \uc2  "The external environment and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market movements since the fourth quarter of 2012 have created a considerably worrying situation," South Korean Deputy Finance Minister Choi Jong-ku told a seminar in Seoul. \par \par \uc2  "The recent wave of quantitative easing policies has created an unprecedented situation and makes it necessary (for affected countries) to adopt a paradigm shift in response," Choi said.\par \par \uc2  The South Korean government will tell state-controlled firms to refrain from borrowing abroad and will further tighten rules on banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives trading to ease volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, Choi said.\par \par \uc2  Seoul was opposed to imposing an outright levy on financial transactions such as the Tobin tax being debated in Europe, but would consider similar measures should speculation in the won \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  intensify over time, he added. \par \par \uc2  Such a tax could discourage speculation but might also reduce normal investment, possibly even causing foreign investors to flee a market en masse.\par \par \uc2  In Bangkok, Finance Minister Kittirat Na Ranong told reporters that Prime Minister \uc2 Yingluck Shinawatra\uc2  was worried about the fallout from advanced economies' easy money policies and has ordered policymakers to discuss ways to deal with it.\par \par \uc2  He said authorities will use normal financial measures to manage flows of money into and out of the country but added there were no plans to use capital controls or taxes. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht is hovering near a 17-month high and has risen close to 3 percent against the U.S. dollar so far this year.\par \par \uc2  Talk about a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war, or competitive devaluation of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at giving exporters an upper hand in pricing goods abroad, dominated discussions at the \uc2 World Economic Forum\uc2  in Davos last week, with many central bankers and business executives questioning the wisdom of such money policy.\par \par \uc2  German Chancellor Angela Merkel last week singled out Japan as a source of concern following recent moves by its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to quicken the pace of money-printing. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  WAR?\par \par \uc2  Authorities in \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 , South Korea, Taiwan and the Philippines have repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in recent months to try to slow the advance of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which they fear could jeopardise their expected economic recoveries.\par \par \uc2  Latin American policymakers have also been bolstering their defences.  \par \par \uc2 Colombia cut interest rates this month and said it would ramp up dollar purchases, Peru plans to pre-pay up to $1.5 billion in foreign debt this year and is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively to curb \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains, and Mexico is considering an interest rate cut that some economists believe reflects as much a desire for a weaker peso as it does concerns about growth. \par \par \uc2  "I sincerely hope other countries do not get into competitive depreciation of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Indian Finance Minister Palaniapan Chidambaram told Reuters Television in London on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2  Chidambaram said it was too early to say Japan's latest round of policy easing earlier this month constituted a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war but expressed strong concerns about such a scenario.\par \par \uc2  "It will hurt us very badly. Our exports are down this year compared to last year because of the global situation...If (there is \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war), our exports will suffer even more." \par \par \uc2  Japanese Prime Minister Shinzo Abe, whose campaign for unlimited money printing has sent the yen plunging, waded into the growing global debate about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars for the first time on Wednesday, shrugging off such criticism. \par \par \uc2  The yen fell 10 percent against the dollar in the final quarter of 2012 alone, its sharpest quarterly loss in 17 years, on expectations the BOJ and a new government would launch additional stimulus.\par \par \uc2  This dealt an especially heavy blow to export-dependent economies in the region such as South Korea and Thailand as their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were firming against the dollar.\par \par \uc2  As a result, the value of the South Korean won and Thai baht against the yen surged 16.5 percent and 12.1 percent, respectively, in the fourth quarter, marking their biggest quarterly gains in nearly 15 years, Thomson Reuters data shows.\par \par \uc2  "I don't think Japan or any specific country will be singled out (at the G20 finance chiefs' meeting), but obviously this is among the top issues taking place in the financial markets and therefore will likely be discussed," a G20 negotiator from an Asian country told Reuters recently.\par \par \uc2  G20 leaders agreed at a summit in Seoul in 2010 to avoid waging competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation and instead to make efforts to ease the current account imbalances identified as one of the causes behind the global financial instability. \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Christine Kim and Leika Kihara in TOKYO; Writing by Choonsik Yoo; Editing by Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 GLOBAL-ECONOMY/EASYMONEY (WRAPUP 1) | C | D | E | M | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | G | RBN | J | ABX | BNX | FUN | SI | AFA | CSA | LBY | RWSA | RWS | REULB | GNS | SNI | RNP | DNP | PGE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130130e91u000nc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
