\par\par \b \uc2 Thai Finance Minister: Baht On Uptrend Due to Larger Capital Inflows From Japan, US\b0\par\par\uc2 193 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2013\par 03:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK--The \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  could, if necessary, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to help stabilize the baht in the short run, but no \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can rely on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to dictate the direction of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Deputy Prime Minister and Finance Minister Kittiratt Na-Ranong said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The baht is currently on an uptrend due to larger capital inflows from the U.S. and Japan as a result of increased money supply in the two major economies, Mr. Kittiratt told reporters at the sideline of an economic seminar.\par \par \uc2 He noted that the strong baht could harm certain sectors and at the same time benefit others. While the firmer baht hurts exports and tourism, it reduces the cost of energy and capital goods imports.\par \par \uc2 The government, however, won't leave the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  to battle the baht's uptrend alone, Mr. Kittiratt said, adding that the government will work to facilitate more overseas investment by Thai investors to counter capital inflows and reduce baht appreciation pressures.\par \par \uc2 Write to djnews.bangkok@dowjones.com [ 23-01-13 0312GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130123e91n0002x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
