\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: \uc2 Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Thailand \highlight\uc2 : Not Planning to  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Market\b0\par\par\uc2 546 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 2012\par 13:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  not planning to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow baht's rise, governor says.\par \par \uc2 -- The BOT sees euro-zone crisis as key risk to Thai economy.\par \par \uc2 -- Thailand's banking system secure, has adequate liquidity to facilitate domestic economic recovery, he says.\par \par \uc2 (Adds exchange-rate updates in second paragraph, BOT's comments in fourth-10th paragraphs, comments from Stock Exchange of Thailand president in 11th-13th paragraphs.)\par \par \uc2 BANGKOK--The \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't planning to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to slow the baht's rise, Gov. Prasarn Trairatvorakul said Monday.\par \par \uc2 Following the electoral victory on Sunday of Greece's pro-bailout New Democracy party, which boosted risk appetite in Asian markets, the U.S. dollar hit a session low of THB31.40 earlier in the day from THB31.51-THB31.52 late Friday in Asia.\par \par \uc2 The greenback had recovered to THB31.47-THB31.48 as at 0933 GMT on short-covering and demand from local corporates.\par \par \uc2 "The baht's movement hasn't been that strong and can be explained," Mr. Prasarn said at a press conference. "We will step in to take care of the situation only when there is any movement that can't be explained fundamentally."\par \par \uc2 He expects the positive market reaction to Greece's election result to drive trading for a day or two while investors await the outcome of the Group of 20 meeting that will end Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Also on Monday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement that the euro zone's prolonged economic crisis poses the main risk to Thailand's economy, and that the situation there may have adverse effects on international money-market stability and the global economy.\par \par \uc2 In the short run, the Europe problem could cause short-term volatility in Thailand's money and capital markets, while liquidity in international money markets could tighten from time to time, the BOT noted in the statement issued after a meeting between the bank's Monetary Policy Committee and Financial Institutions Policy Committee.\par \par \uc2 The two committees have discussed policy options to cope with the impact from the euro-zone crisis on the local economy and financial system, and stand ready to deal with any potential fallout, the statement said.\par \par \uc2 However, "[the discussion] was more of an analysis [of the situation]" and the policy tools have yet to be implemented, Mr. Prasarn said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said that the Thai banking system remains secure and that it has adequate liquidity to facilitate a domestic economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Separately, Stock Exchange of Thailand President Charamporn Jotikasthira said Monday that the Thai stock market is likely to be highly volatile over the next one to two months following Greece's pro-bailout New Democracy party's victory.\par \par \uc2 Like most Asian bourses, Thai shares were up, by 0.8% at 1175.42 around midday, as worries over Greece's potential exit from the euro-zone bloc eased after New Democracy won the election.\par \par \uc2 Investors should tread cautiously as the rally may not last amid lingering concerns about the new Greek government's policy and the size of the new bailout package that the country will receive, Mr. Charamporn told reporters.