\par\par \b \uc2 TEXT: Fitch Affirms CIMB Thai Bank; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 718 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2011\par 07:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (The following was released by the rating agency)\par \par \uc2 BANGKOK/HONG KONG/SINGAPORE, November 24 (Fitch) Fitch Ratings has today affirmed \uc2 CIMB Thai Bank Public Company Limited\uc2 's (CIMBT) Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR at 'BBB' and its National Long-Term Rating at 'AA-(tha)'. The Outlook is Stable. A full rating breakdown is provided below.\par \par \uc2 CIMBT's ratings are primarily driven by Fitch's view that there is a high probability of support, if required, from its parent, CIMB Bank Berhad (CIMB; 'BBB+'/Stable), the fifth-largest financial services group in Southeast Asia. CIMB has a 93.15% stake in CIMBT and plays a key role in the latter's transformation programme and balance sheet clean-up. Also, CIMBT has aligned its strategy with CIMB's regional franchise network, focusing on cross-border and cross-business opportunities. CIMB's late-2010 THB3.0bn capital injection will support medium term growth and provides cushion against global and domestic market volatility.\par \par \uc2 Any changes in CIMB's ability and/or propensity to support (including lowering its stake) could affect CIMBT's Long-Term ratings. As this is not Fitch's expectation, CIMBT's Outlook is Stable, consistent with its parent's.\par \par \uc2 The above improvements are reflected in the affirmation of CIMBT's Viability Rating of 'bb-', which also takes into account the bank's modest franchise and liquidity relative to higher-rated institutions in Thailand. A positive rating action is dependent on sustained strengthening of its franchise and liquidity, with further improvements in profitability, asset quality and capital. A negative rating action could stem from aggressive expansion if it compromises credit quality and its funding profile.\par \par \uc2 CIMBT's core profitability has improved with net interest income (NII) increasing 5% yoy in 2010 and 10% yoy in 9M11, from solid loan growth (2010: 9% yoy; 9M11: 19.6% ytd), mainly in corporate loans and retail lending (residential mortgages loans and auto hire purchases). Despite high growth, Tier 1 and total capital ratios remain adequate at 8.1% and 13.8%, respectively, at end-September 2011, while liquidity remains reasonable with a loan-to-deposit ratio (included bills of exchange) of 88.5% at end-June 2011. CIMBT also has strong funding and capital commitment from CIMB, as evidenced by its subscription of CIMBT's upper- and lower-tier 2 instruments.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless asset quality remains a key concern with a high level of impaired loans. Its NPL rose to THB3.8bn or 3.4% of total loans at end-September 2011 (end-2010: THB2.9bn or 3.1%) due to a deterioration in asset quality of certain corporate loans, while special mention loans remain high at 7.8% of total loans at end-June 2011 (end-2010: 9.3%). Fitch views that the major flooding in Thailand since September 2011 could weaken CIMBT's delinquency rates over the next six months, which will increase provisioning risk and impact its profitability in 2012.\par \par \uc2 CIMBT's hybrid upper tier 2 debt rating is notched two levels below the bank's National Long-Term Rating. Despite coupon deferral mechanisms, Fitch believes CIMB would still support coupon payments. In the event CIMBT's capital adequacy ratio falls below 0%, or if \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 , this would result in mandatory deferral. Fitch however considers the risk of this as low, in light of expectations of timely support from the parent. CIMBT's lower-tier 2 debt is rated one notch below the bank's National Long-Term Rating, consistent with Fitch's rating criteria.\par \par \uc2 CIMBT, formerly Bank Thai (BT), was formed in 1998 as a result of a government-initiated merger of several defunct financial institutions. It is one of the smallest banks in Thailand, with asset size and deposits share of 1.4% at end-June 2011.\par \par \uc2 CIMBT's ratings:\par \par \uc2 - Long-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR affirmed at 'BBB'; Outlook Stable;\par \par \uc2 - Short-Term Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR affirmed at 'F3';\par \par \uc2 - Viability Rating affirmed at 'bb-';\par \par \uc2 - Individual Rating affirmed at 'D';\par \par \uc2 - Support Rating affirmed at '2';\par \par \uc2 - National Long-Term Rarting affirmed at 'AA-(tha)'; Outlook Stable;\par \par \uc2 - National Short-Term Rating affirmed at 'F1+(tha)';\par \par \uc2 - Upper tier 2 debt affirmed at 'A(tha)'; and\par \par \uc2 - Lower-tier 2 debt affirmed at 'A+(tha)'.\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-RATINGS-CIMB   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111127e7bo0003x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
