\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Fund redemptions hit baht, Philippine peso\b0\par\par\uc2 1,160 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2011\par 04:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Equity market outflows hurt baht, peso\par \par \uc2 * Won gives up Friday's gains on lack of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Indonesia, Malaysia spotted\par \par \uc2 (Updates text, prices)\par \par \uc2 By Jongwoo Cheon\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Sept 26 (Reuters) - The Thai baht and Philippine peso fell on Monday, with talk of equity fund redemptions driving investors to sell off what had been two outperforming Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the year.\par \par \uc2 Authorities in Thailand, Indonesia and Malaysia were battling the market on Monday, selling U.S. dollars from their reserve stockpiles to protect their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though South Korea notably did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The outlook on emerging markets globally is dark as most dealers are looking for chances to sell those units on rebounds on persistent worries about the euro zone's sovereign crisis. A sharp decline in emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has preceded broader weakness in risky assets.\par \par \uc2 "I will keep buying dollar/Asia on dips as we have not seen signs of an end in the crisis yet," said a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at a European bank in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 "Asian authorities are expected to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be a just temporary tool, which will not be able to stop the trend."\par \par \uc2 Emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been pressured by dollar-demand in non-deliverable forwards (NDFs) from leveraged and institutional investors, who have over the past few weeks rushed to hedge against more local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness. Some funds were building up dollar-long positions against the regional units.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the Singapore dollar, once the safe-haven in Asia, posted the largest weekly percentage fall by losing 4.6 percent against the U.S. dollar, according to Reuters' calculation based on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data. The Indian rupee\par \par \uc2 also saw the worst week in more than 15 years.\par \par \uc2 The losses came as other emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and growth-linked units broadly fell. The Brazil's real lost 7.5 percent last week, although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sale of $2.75 billion of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps limited its falls.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the Australian dollar also fell to a 10-month low against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 WON\par \par \uc2 The won slid to a one-year low versus the greenback, wiping out Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 -driven gains, without signs of dollar-selling by the authorities.\par \par \uc2 The South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended local trade at 1,195.8, slightly stronger than Friday's 1,196.0, the softest since early Sept last year.\par \par \uc2 Earlier, the won had found some relief from bond inflows, but investors dumped the unit again with no sign of authorities.\par \par \uc2 "There is a consensus that the authorities will defend 1,200 after last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But people are using strategies to buy it (dollar/won) until levels where the BOK is seen and sell it there, given no sellers except the BOK," said a trading head of a foreign bank in Seoul.\par \par \uc2 After domestic market closed, Deputy Finance Minister Choi Jong-ku said the country will closely monitor any speculative trade behind the recent won's falls.\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 Macro funds, leveraged accounts and U.S. investment banks pushed the Singapore dollar down to near the 50 percent retracement level of its appreciation between June 2010 and July this year.\par \par \uc2 The city-state's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also suffered from stops in U.S. dollar/Singapore dollar above 1.3050, while macro funds bought the pair from 1.2915, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 BAHT, PHILIPPINE PESO\par \par \uc2 The Thai baht and the Philippine peso\par \par \uc2 suffered from fund redemption, which hit local stocks, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are expected to weaken more.\par \par \uc2 Thai stocks and Philippine shares tumbled a near 9 percent and more 4 percent, respectively.\par \par \uc2 The baht weakened to as soft as 31.26 per dollar, the softest since late January, breaching the support of 31.00, the level which was where a rejection of dollar/baht's rally was seen just ahead of the Thai election earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 If the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stays weaker than that 31.00, it is seen weakening to 31.21-31.29. The 31.29 is the low of Jan 31 and 31.21 is a 100-week moving average.\par \par \uc2 The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spotted selling dollars from 31.05 level, dealers said, while real money funds kept selling it.\par \par \uc2 A deputy governor said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to act on excessive move in the baht.\par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso shed as much as 0.7 percent, weakening past the 61.8 percent retracement of its appreciation between January and August.\par \par \uc2 The peso may head to 44.02, the session's low of 44.02 per dollar on March 17, while it also has the 76.4 percent retracement around the level.\par \par \uc2 RINGGIT\par \par \uc2 The ringgit lost 0.7 percent against the dollar as interbank speculators continued to build up dollar-long positions.\par \par \uc2 "We stay long as the euro is below 1.3460," said a Kuala Lumpur-based dealer.\par \par \uc2 But the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spotted selling dollars to limit falls in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen at 3.1820 per dollar earlier and it moved dollar offers to 3.1880 and 3.1900 later, they added.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 Change on the day at 0725 GMT \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        76.25          76.58       +0.43\par  Sing dlr        1.3057         1.2979       -0.60\par  Taiwan dlr      30.586         30.388       -0.65\par  Korean won     1194.40        1166.00       -2.38\par  Baht             31.13          30.86       -0.87\par  Peso             43.83          43.58       -0.57\par  Rupiah         9100.00        8870.00       -2.53\par  Rupee            49.61          49.44       -0.33\par  Ringgit         3.1890         3.1662       -0.71\par  Yuan            6.3892         6.3889       -0.00\par  Change so far in 2011\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        76.25          81.15       +6.43\par  Sing dlr        1.3057         1.2820       -1.82\par  Taiwan dlr      30.586         30.368       -0.71\par  Korean won     1194.40        1134.80       -4.99\par  Baht             31.13          30.14       -3.18\par  Peso             43.83          43.84       +0.02\par  Rupiah         9100.00        9005.00       -1.04\par  Rupee            49.61          44.70       -9.89\par  Ringgit         3.1890         3.0820       -3.36\par  Yuan            6.3892         6.5897       +3.14\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Lim Seunggyu in SEOUL, Reuters \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analysts Krishna Kumar, Rick Lloyd, and Catherine Tan of IFR Markets; Editing by Kevin Plumberg)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the new Reuters scrolling global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  service please click\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Double click on brackets for following items: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2      Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Asia \tab \par Malaysian ringgit   Indonesian rupiah  \tab \par Singapore dollar    Thai baht          \tab \par Taiwan dollar       Hong Kong dollar   \tab \par Philippine peso      Korean won         \tab \par SPEED GUIDES  \tab \par Exotic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2      Non-G7 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   \tab \par RELATED NEWS AND OTHER TOPICS \tab \par All Singapore news    All Malaysian news \tab \par Thailand Indonesia Hong Kong Taiwan \tab \par Philippines Korea All emerging markets \tab \par All \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news    \tab \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  technical analysis    [FRX&INSI]\tab \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110929e79q00045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
